# Task: batchnorm1d
Inference BatchNorm (1d) with statistics folded offline (benchmarks/batchnorm_ckks): y = x * A + B.
Inputs: ciphertext `x`; plaintext vectors `A` and `B`, one value per slot giving each channel's scale and shift.
Output: `y`. Any equivalent evaluation is allowed.

Correctness: every output must be within 1e-06 (absolute) of the expected values on all test cases (fixed, dense). Timing is measured on the dense case.

# Baseline kernel
```cpp
// Baseline generated by agent/tasks/gen_baselines.py; y = x * A + B, as in benchmarks/batchnorm_ckks (BatchNorm1d and BatchNorm2d).

void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext y = x * a + b;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

# Baseline result
status: ok (correct on every case)
fhe() evaluation time (dense case, median): 18.21 ms
CKKS levels consumed: 1
CHEHAB depth (total, multiplicative): (2, 1); IR terms: 6
operations: ct_ct_mul=0, ct_pt_mul=1, relin=0, rotate=0, rescale=1, mod_switch=0, match_scale=0, add=1
max abs error vs expected: 2.33e-08

You have 8 attempts. Propose a faster kernel that computes the same outputs.

The baseline is already close to minimal: one ct-pt multiply with rescale, plus one plaintext add. The plaintext add costs about 2.5 ms for encoding, and the multiply costs about 7 ms. The measured 18 ms probably includes the encoding of A and B.

I don't see a cheaper algebraic form, since each operand has to be used at least once. The only change I can try is the order of operations. Computing `(x + b/a) * a` would need a plaintext division, which isn't allowed. So I keep the same computation and make only a small structural change: the add happens after the rescale, at the deeper level, which is cheaper. That is already what the baseline does.

I'm submitting the baseline form unchanged, as there is no clear improvement to make.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext y = x * a + b;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

The kernel is already at the minimum operation count: one ct-pt multiply and one plaintext add. I can't find a cheaper algebraic form, so I'm trying a variation in how the operations are written. Here the add is written as an explicit `+=` on a copy of the product. CHEHAB should compile this to the same graph, so I expect little or no change in time.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext y = x * a;
  y += b;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

The two forms compile to the same graph, so the 1 ms difference between attempts 1 and 2 is most likely measurement noise. Every operand has to be used once, so I can't cut an operation.

One idea is to add b before the multiply at the first level, so the add happens in the ciphertext domain. That would need b/a, which isn't allowed. Another is to add b after the rescale, which is what already happens. Both are dead ends, so I'm resubmitting the best form, the original baseline.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext y = x * a + b;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

The kernel is at the minimum operation count: one ct-pt multiply (with rescale) and one plaintext add. The differences between attempts (18.14, 18.21, 18.29 ms) look like noise, and I don't see an algebraic rewrite that removes either operation.

I'm trying one structural variation. Writing `x * a + b` as `(x + b') * a` isn't possible without a plaintext division. So I'm submitting the same minimal computation again, written as `b + x * a`. It should compile to the same graph, and any timing difference would only be noise.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext y = x * a + b;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

I need to correct my last message. I said I was submitting `b + x * a`, but the code was identical to the baseline, so the 18.42 ms result was a repeat run and shows only measurement noise (18.14 to 18.42 ms across identical graphs).

The graph is already minimal. It has one ct-pt multiply with rescale and one plaintext add, and each plaintext input must be encoded and used once. Without constants or plaintext-plaintext arithmetic, I can't fold `b` into the ciphertext side or remove an operation. This attempt really does swap the operand order, `b + x * a`. I expect the same compiled graph and a time within noise.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext y = x * a;
  y = b + y;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

This attempt, `b + x * a`, gave 18.34 ms, which is within noise of the others. All five runs compile to the same graph (1 ct-pt multiply, 1 rescale, 1 add) and land between 18.14 and 18.42 ms.

I see no remaining way to cut work. Both plaintexts must be encoded and used once, and the rules forbid constants and plaintext-plaintext arithmetic, so `b` can't be pre-merged with `a`. Any rewrite either adds operations or compiles to the same thing. I'm resubmitting the best-measured form, the baseline expression, and I don't expect a real gain from further attempts.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext y = x * a + b;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

This run took 19.90 ms with the same code as the 18.14 ms run, so the spread is noise from the machine, not from the kernel. The graph is still 1 ct-pt multiply, 1 rescale and 1 add, and I have no valid rewrite that reduces it. I'm submitting the baseline expression again as the best-measured form.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext y = x * a + b;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

The last two runs (19.90 and 20.29 ms) used code identical to the 18.14 ms run, so the variation is system noise. The graph is already minimal, with one ct-pt multiply and rescale plus one plaintext add, and I have no rewrite that removes an operation. For the final attempt I'm submitting the same baseline expression.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext y = x * a + b;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```
